our monsoon season has brought plenty of  rain to our valley and while we need the  water desperately it has also caused  some major damage residents in one  sunrise manor neighborhood are telling  us it's been a constant issue with  floods debris and other problems  i don't know if we're going to do  sandbags or  what we're going to do but we're going  to do something to divert the water kim  kelsey has been a sunrise manor resident  for 26 years she enjoys the open land  but she says that comes with a price  when storms roll by  i don't like when it rips out our trees  and takes our power out three years ago  it took it out for five days the most  recent storms caused flooding inside her  property you can see her wood floors  covered by mud and damaged by the water  it was probably  maybe a foot and a half deep there you  know on the house and then my downstairs  got flooded my yard my sidewalk  everything got flooded if you look out  front there you can see where the water  came  the rock hard soil combined with the  heat allows rain when it happens to  quickly turn into raging rivers  according to the regional flood control  district we spotted several streets near  hollywood boulevard and charleston with  dried mud and debris from recent storms  these rocks are normally red we've  cleaned most of them off because it was  a big mess to come home to but you can  see they're all brown now i still have  to wash more  as the cleanup process continues our 8  news now weather team is forecasting  more storms to roll into the valley a  concern for kelsey both safety and  financially it's going to be thousands  of dollars i'm going to have to redo  floor and walls and  everything i still don't even know  exactly how it got in i think it just  got so high on the house  that it just seeped right through the  stucco  well we reached out to the regional  flood control district about this issue  and they tell us there is a project that  is under designed right now to provide  some relief no other details were  provided but they recommend flood  insurance and contacting the county for  ways to protect your property
 

